<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jacobus Arminius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1560">1560</a>–<a href="/years/1609">1609</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Jakob Hermanszoon, Arminius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jacobus <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arminius">Arminius</a>, born Jakob Hermanszoon in Oudewater, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> Reformed minister and theologian whose views became the basis of Arminianism and the Dutch Remonstrant movement. He became an orphan while still young, and studied at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leiden">Leiden</a> from 1576 to 1582 and then under Theodore Beza at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> and at Basel. From 1603 he was professor of theology at the University of Leiden and wrote many books and treatises. After his death in 1609, his challenge to the Belgic Confession was debated at the Synod of Dort, which crafted the five points of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvinism">Calvinism</a> in response to his teaching.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jacobus-arminius.jpg" alt="Jacobus Arminius" width="309" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Bailly · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:James_Arminius_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
